  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Tuesday...\n\nThe region remains under an area of weak flow aloft with a strong\nupper low situated to the south and ridging nosing in from the \nwest. Temperatures will drop into the 20s and 30s into the morning\nhours, with patchy fog forecast to develop over portions of the \ncentral and east. With this, locally dense fog is possible at \ntimes. Make sure to use caution if traveling into this morning as \nvisibility could change rapidly. \n\nAs the aforementioned low tracks east into the central Rockies, an\nassociated wave lifting north and east through Wyoming looks to\nbring showers (15-30% chance) to the far southeast this afternoon\nand evening. Any precipitation will be light. Temperatures will\nnudge higher today, reaching the 50s to lower 60s for most\nlocations. Behind the wave, a dry cold front will move through \nthe area tonight, bringing breezy northwest winds to the eastern \nhalf of the area on Thursday, along with slightly cooler\nconditions (highs in the 50s). Wind gusts of 15 to 30 mph will be\ncommon, with gusts into the 40s mph along the Dakotas border.\n \nHigh pressure building over the region will bring generally warmer\nconditions from Friday through early next week with highs \nincreasing into the 60s to 70s, warmest on Saturday. Breezy \nconditions are forecast at times, with pressure gradients \ntightening along the western foothills Friday night into Saturday\nmorning (gap winds), and another dry cold front passage Saturday \nnight/Sunday morning bringing the potential for more wind on \nSunday. WPC cluster analysis and ensembles point to a break down \nof the ridge and large scale troughing developing over the western\nUS by the middle of next week. This could bring a return to \ncooler and unsettled conditions by Tuesday or Wednesday. STP",
